details.image_prompt:
[
  "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The scene is composed at a 3/4 overhead angle, ensuring all components are clearly visible while maintaining a shallow depth of field where the foreground bowl is sharp and the secondary serving area is softly blurred. Soft, natural side light enters from a window on the left, creating gentle shadows and highlighting the creamy texture of the dip. The setting is a rustic, aged oak tabletop with slight natural wood grain and a few scattered pumpkin seeds. The centerpiece is a simple matte white ceramic bowl filled with a smooth, pale-amber butter bean dip, topped with a generous sprinkle of roasted dark-green pepitas and a dusting of vibrant red smoked paprika. Beside the bowl, arranged on a linen napkin, are crisp, bright red bell pepper strips and fresh orange carrot sticks, placed side-by-side to ensure each is clearly identifiable. A wooden spoon rests casually near the bowl. The arrangement is authentic and lived-in, featuring a tiny smear of dip on the rim and a few stray pepper seeds on the table, reflecting the cooking process. The lighting is warm and golden-hour inspired, making the dip look velvety and the vegetables look snappy and fresh. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene should look like someone just finished cooking and plating — lived-in, warm, authentic. Not a sterile studio setup. Slight natural imperfections: an asymmetric garnish placement, a small smear of dip on the rim, slightly uneven vegetable cuts. Include subtle traces of the cooking process: a few scattered pepitas on the surface, a slight oil mark near the bowl. No plastic sheen, no unnaturally smooth surfaces, no perfect symmetry. Warm, golden-hour color temperature — cozy and inviting, not cold or clinical. No text, no watermark, no logo, no hands, no faces. The image should be indistinguishable from a photograph taken by a professional food photographer for a printed cookbook."
]
